# Board item 177 (2026-10-01): the FREE safety work gets its own schedule.
[Unit]
Description=quant-agent intra_safety — free broker-truth safety preamble (fill reconcile, stop-out reconcile, protection-restore drain, repeg drain) on its OWN schedule, independent of the paid intraday tick

[Timer]
# NO NUMBER WAS INVENTED HERE. Both halves of this expression are copied
# from something that already exists:
#
#   INTERVAL — 30 minutes, which is `src.config.INTRA_CHECK_TICK_MINUTES`,
#   an operator-set value carrying `status: sourced` in
#   config/number_ledger.yaml. This work already runs on that interval
#   today, as the opening block of the paid intra_check tick; keeping it
#   there changes the protection latency by exactly nothing, which is the
#   point — the schedule becomes explicit without becoming different.
#
#   PHASE — :07:30 / :37:30 is the MIDPOINT of the two phases already in
#   use on this box: every other session timer is on *:0/30 and
#   quant-agent-intra_check.timer is on *:15,45 (moved there 2026-09-17 to
#   stop same-second races; see that unit's header and
#   docs/INCIDENT_HISTORY.md). Half of that 15-minute spacing is 7m30s.
#   The midpoint is the only phase at this interval that shares a second
#   with no existing unit, and it is derived from the two existing phases
#   rather than picked.
#
# What would let someone choose a DIFFERENT interval: nothing in the
# record can justify one yet. The honest input would be the observed
# distribution of how long a position actually stays unprotected — i.e.
# the gap between a protection-restore intent being written and the drain
# clearing it, and between a broker-side stop-out filling and the
# reconciler writing it back. Those timestamps are persisted
# (pending_protection_restores, the stop-out reconcile rows) but have
# never been measured as a latency distribution. Until they are, a
# tighter or looser number would be a guess.
OnCalendar=*:07,37:30
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
